Sure, here's a Java method that takes a `List<Car>` and returns the count and color of the most popular `Car.getColor`:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class CarCounter {
    public static Map<String, Integer> getMostPopularCar(List<Car> cars) {
        Map<String, Integer> colorCount = new HashMap<>();

        for (Car car : cars) {
            String color = car.getColor();
            colorCount.put(color, colorCount.getOrDefault(color, 0) + 1);
        }

        String mostPopularColor = null;
        int maxCount = 0;

        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : colorCount.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() > maxCount) {
                mostPopularColor = entry.getKey();
                maxCount = entry.getValue();
            }
        }

        return Map.of(mostPopularColor, maxCount);
    }
}
```

This method first counts the occurrences of each car color using a `HashMap`. It then iterates through the map to find the color with the highest count and returns it along with its count in a `Map<String, Integer>`.

